--- body ---
## Context
Found during raid 219-224 (2026-06-04) by the 0222 execute agent: a
stray src/go/git-erg binary left by `go build ./...` made the
offline-invariant check in tests/test_update.sh FAIL -- the grep for
net/http or crypto/tls scans src/go/ recursively and matched the
binary's debug symbols. The official build (make) strips symbols with
-s -w and outputs to build/erg, so it never produces that file, but
any developer or agent running `go build ./...` inside src/go/ trips
the suite. Test-isolation sharp edge, not a product defect.

## Actions
1. Restrict the offline-invariant grep in tests/test_update.sh to
   *.go files (e.g. `grep -rn --include='*.go' ...`), or exclude
   non-text/gitignored files.

## Test
With a fake binary file containing the bytes "net/http" planted in
src/go/, the suite passes; with a real `import "net/http"` added to a
.go file, the suite fails.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Stray compiled binary in src/go/ no longer fails test_update.sh
- [ ] The invariant still catches a real net/http import in a .go file
- [ ] make check passes

## Imagine (raid 2026-06-04)
- Why now: the 0222 execute agent hit a live false-positive during the current raid; the sharp edge is active and reproducible whenever any developer or agent runs `go build ./...` inside src/go/ (produces a binary with embedded stdlib symbol strings including "net/http"), while the guarded make-build outputs only to build/erg (stripped -s -w, never in src/go/). No prior guard existed.
- Scope check: the broken grep scans all of src/go/ recursively; src/go/assets/ holds only text files (.ergrc, AGENTS.md, *.md), but future build artifacts landing there would re-trigger the false positive -- restricting to *.go files is therefore forward-safe and tightly scoped. test_contract.sh already guards the semantic invariant via `go list -deps` (requires Go toolchain); this ticket guards only the textual-grep false-positive path.
- Simplest path: add `--include='*.go'` to the one grep in test_update.sh line 208; one-line change, no new helpers, matches the idiom used in test_determinism.sh (`grep -n ... src/go/*.go`). The negative-control self-test in the Actions section (plant fake bytes vs real import) is sufficient; no test-framework overhead needed.
- Antipattern scan: clean -- no hand-rolled abstraction, no duplication of the go-list check that already lives in test_contract.sh, no scope beyond the one broken line.

## Plan (raid 2026-06-04)
- Actions: tests/test_update.sh:208 -- change `grep -rEn 'net/http|crypto/tls' src/go/` to `grep -rEn --include='*.go' 'net/http|crypto/tls' src/go/`. One line.
- First test: plant a binary file containing the bytes "net/http" in src/go/ (cp /bin/true + append string), run the suite block -- must PASS; add `import "net/http"` to a scratch .go copy -- grep must match (negative control per ticket Test section, demonstrated in PR body, not committed).
- Dependencies: none. GNU grep --include is available on CI (ubuntu) and dev hosts.
- Tautology scan: clean -- the guard still greps real source; correction: the Imagine note cited a test_determinism.sh precedent for --include, but no such idiom exists in tests/ (grep confirms 0 hits); this is the first use, which is fine.
